          <a href="#compared" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. How Horizon Compares With the Brands It Is Shopped Against</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Who Should Buy a Horizon, and Who Should Not</a>
          <a href="#what-to-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. If You Are Shopping for a Horizon</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          "Is Horizon a good brand?" usually means one of three things: is it well made, will it
          be looked after if something goes wrong, and is it a sensible way to avoid paying a monthly
          fee for a treadmill. The short version is yes on the third, a qualified yes on the first two,
          and the qualifications are specific enough to be worth reading before you spend a thousand
          dollars. This page sets out who makes Horizon, what its approach gets right, where it falls
          short, what happened with the 2022 recall, and which buyers it suits.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is Horizon a Good Treadmill Brand? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes, for most home users who want a straightforward treadmill without a
            subscription: Horizon pairs a lifetime frame and motor warranty with open Bluetooth app
            support, at prices that usually sit around or below $1,000 on sale.</strong> It is owned by
            Johnson Health Tech, one of the largest fitness equipment manufacturers in the world, which
            also makes the commercial Matrix brand and, since 2024, Bowflex.
          </p>
          <p>
            The catches are real but specific. Horizon does not publish a motor horsepower rating for
            any current treadmill, so you cannot compare its motors on paper. Its parts and labour cover
            is much shorter than the lifetime headline suggests, and labour runs out after one or two
            years. Apps can read a Horizon but cannot control it, so classes will not change your
            incline for you. And in October 2022 about 192,000 older T101-05 treadmills were recalled
            because they could speed up, slow down or stop on their own; in August 2026 the importer
            agreed to a $16.875 million civil penalty, provisionally accepted by the CPSC, over
            reporting that hazard late.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that makes Horizon a bad choice. It makes it a brand you should buy with your eyes
            open: choose the model by its deck, weight rating and parts warranty, check the serial
            number on any older or used T101, and accept that you will set the incline yourself. If you
            have already decided Horizon suits you, our ranking of the
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Horizon treadmills</a>
            goes model by model through the current range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-horizon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Makes Horizon Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Horizon Fitness belongs to Johnson Health Tech, a manufacturer headquartered in Taichung,
            Taiwan, whose corporate site marks fifty years in business. Its North American headquarters
            is in Cottage Grove, Wisconsin. Horizon is one of several brands the group runs at different
            price points: Matrix is its commercial and premium line, the kind of equipment you see in
            hotel and club gyms; Vision sits in specialty fitness stores; and Bowflex joined the group
            when Johnson Health Tech acquired it in 2024. Johnson Fitness &amp; Wellness, the group's own
            chain of stores, sells several of these brands side by side.
          </p>
          <p>
            Why does that matter to someone buying a $900 treadmill? For three practical reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Scale and continuity.</strong> A large manufacturer that makes its own machines is
            more likely to still be answering the phone, and still selling parts, in year eight than a
            marketplace brand that exists only as a storefront. That is not a guarantee, but it is the
            single best predictor of whether a lifetime warranty means anything. A lifetime frame
            warranty from a company that disappears in three years is worth nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Shared engineering.</strong> Brands inside one group tend to share suppliers,
            drive systems and design thinking. Horizon's Go Series uses what Horizon calls the Johnson
            Drive System, and the Bowflex T6, now made by the same parent company, lines up closely with
            the Horizon 7.0 AT on deck, speed, incline and capacity. If you are cross-shopping the two,
            you are partly comparing styling, consoles and warranty terms rather than fundamentally
            different machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Accountability runs to the parent.</strong> The flip side is that the parent
            company's record is part of the brand's record. The recall and the civil penalty discussed
            below were matters for Johnson Health Tech's US trading company, and the CPSC's statement on
            the 2026 settlement noted that the firm had previously paid two civil penalties for similar
            reporting failures. A buyer weighing Horizon fairly should weigh that too, alongside the
            strengths.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="open-app-approach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The No-Subscription, Open-App Approach</h2>
<p>
            Horizon's defining choice is what it does not build. NordicTrack and ProForm design their
            machines around iFIT, Peloton around its own membership, and those platforms are a recurring
            cost for as long as you want the screen and the automatic speed and incline control to do
            their job. Horizon has no platform of its own. Every current Horizon treadmill has a console
            that works fully on its own — programs, dials, heart rate — and a Bluetooth radio that sends
            speed, incline and distance to whichever app you already use.
          </p>
          <p>
            That radio uses Bluetooth FTMS, the open standard for fitness machines, which is what Zwift,
            the Peloton app, Kinomap and similar apps read. The practical benefits for a brand
            assessment are bigger than they first look.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Nothing to stop working.</strong> A treadmill built around a single company's
            platform depends on that company keeping the software and the subscription going. When a
            platform changes its terms, raises its price or drops older consoles, the machine loses part
            of what you paid for. A Horizon with no app at all is still a complete treadmill, and an app
            you stop liking can be swapped for another without changing the machine. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            covers this software-obsolescence problem in more detail; Horizon largely sidesteps it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lower lifetime cost.</strong> A subscription costing a few hundred dollars a year
            adds up, over the life of a treadmill, to more than the price gap between Horizon's cheapest
            and most expensive models. Horizon's argument is that it puts that money into the frame and
            the warranty instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The trade-off is control.</strong> Because apps receive data from a Horizon rather
            than control it, a class instructor or a Zwift climb cannot raise the incline for you. You
            turn the dial yourself. Horizon's dial controls make that quick, but if automatic control is
            what keeps you training, a Horizon is the wrong brand regardless of its other strengths. Only
            the T101 Connect lists JRNY, the adaptive-workout service that came to the group with Bowflex,
            and it is optional. Our roundup of
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            sets Horizon against the other brands that take the same approach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-pattern" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Warranty Pattern: Generous at the Top, Short Underneath</h2>
<p>
            Horizon's warranty is the reason it gets recommended so often, and it is also the part most
            often misread. The structure is consistent across the current range, which makes it easy to
            describe.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model group</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Frame and motor</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Parts</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Labour</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Go Series (T101 Connect, T202)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Studio Series 7.0 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Studio Series 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Terms are for home use, as listed by Horizon on 13 October 2026. Read the table from right to
            left and the picture changes. The lifetime column is genuinely generous at this price: many
            brands selling machines under $1,000 cover the motor for a few years at most. But the frame
            and the drive motor are the parts least likely to fail on a well-designed treadmill. The
            parts that do fail in the first few years — console electronics, the motor control board,
            the incline motor, the speed sensor, the belt and deck — fall under the parts term, and on
            the Go Series that is one year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Labour is the other half. Once it runs out, a technician's visit is at your expense even when
            the part is free, and on most Horizons that happens at the first anniversary. That is normal
            for the market, but it means "lifetime warranty" and "a lifetime of free repairs" are very
            different things.
          </p>
          <p>
            The useful conclusion for a brand assessment is that Horizon's warranty is strongest where
            you need it least and ordinary where you need it most, and that the gap closes as you move up
            the range. The step from a Go Series machine to the 7.0 AT triples the parts cover, which is
            often the best value in the whole line-up. Horizon's product pages also describe a 30-day
            return for a full refund; confirm who pays for collection before relying on it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read component terms like these across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="range-shape" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What the Range Says About the Brand</h2>
<p>
            You can learn a lot about a brand from the shape of its range, and Horizon's is unusually
            tidy: five current treadmills in two series, with no premium touchscreen flagship and nothing
            built around a content platform.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Go Series is the walking tier.</strong> The T101 Connect and T202 are lighter,
            lower and simpler machines, with thinner belts, the lowest step-on heights in the range and
            one-year parts cover. They suit walking, incline walking and some jogging.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Studio Series is the running tier.</strong> The 7.0 AT, 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT share a
            60-inch deck, 12 mph and 15% incline, and step up in belt width, weight rating and warranty.
            The top of the range is the 7.8 AT at a 375 lb maximum user weight, which is also the highest
            rating Horizon offers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, Horizon competes on the physical machine and the warranty
            rather than on screens, so the money in a Horizon goes into the deck, the frame and the cover
            rather than into a large display. Second, the ceiling is modest. There is no decline, no
            incline above 15%, no commercial-grade home model and nothing rated above 375 lb. That is a
            deliberate position — Horizon is a mid-market home brand — and buyers who need more than
            that ceiling should look elsewhere rather than stretch a Horizon. Heavier users in
            particular will find machines rated higher in our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Stock is the other thing to know about the brand in practice. On the day our roundup was
            checked, only one Studio model was ready to ship, two were a fortnight out, the T202 was a
            month out and the T101 Connect was sold out. That pattern is common in autumn and is not a
            sign of a problem with the machines, but it is part of the experience of buying from Horizon
            directly, and it is why the roundup reports ship dates rather than dropping models that are
            temporarily unavailable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weaknesses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Horizon Does Less Well</h2>
<p>
            A fair assessment of any brand needs the weaknesses stated as clearly as the strengths.
            Horizon's are mostly about information and service rather than build.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">It does not publish motor ratings</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon describes the drive on its Studio Series as a RapidSync motor and on its Go Series as
            a Johnson Drive System, and it lists the thrust of the incline motor. It does not publish a
            horsepower or continuous-duty rating for any current treadmill. Reviewers quote figures from
            2.5 to 4.0 horsepower across the range, and some say openly that the number comes from their
            own research rather than from Horizon. We do not print a figure we cannot trace to the
            manufacturer, so on our pages the motor line is simply blank. The practical effect is that
            you cannot compare a Horizon's motor with a Sole's or a NordicTrack's on paper, and you have
            to judge the machine on deck, weight rating and warranty instead. The lifetime motor warranty
            is reassuring, but it is not the same thing as a published rating. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why continuous ratings matter and how to read them where they exist.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Labour cover is short</h3>
          <p>
            One year of labour on four of the five models, two on the top two. That is in line with the
            market, but next to a lifetime headline it can come as a surprise, and it is the term that
            decides what a repair actually costs you in year three.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Apps cannot drive the machine</h3>
          <p>
            This is a design choice rather than a defect, but it is a limitation. If you want an
            instructor or a virtual route to change your incline automatically, Horizon will frustrate
            you.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Installation constraints</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon's specification tables say its treadmills need a 120V outlet and are not compatible
            with GFCI outlets, which are common in garages, basements and bathrooms. That is not unusual
            among treadmills, but it catches out buyers who planned to put the machine in a garage.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Safety reporting on the record</h3>
          <p>
            The 2022 recall was resolved with a software update, but the August 2026 penalty concerned
            the speed of reporting the hazard rather than the fix. For a buyer, the reasonable reading is
            that the product problem was addressed, and that the company's record on telling the
            regulator promptly is a legitimate black mark. The next section sets out the facts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The 2022 T101-05 Recall, Stated Plainly</h2>
<p>
            Any honest answer to "is Horizon a good brand" has to cover this, and it is better stated from
            the regulator's own notices than from forum summaries.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What was recalled.</strong> On 27 October 2022 the US Consumer Product Safety
            Commission and Johnson Health Tech Trading jointly announced a recall of Horizon Fitness
            T101-05 folding treadmills. The recall covered about 192,000 units sold in the US, plus about
            7,000 in Canada, sold from March 2018 through October 2022 for between about $600 and $1,000.
            Only units with serial numbers beginning TM734 (made in China) or TM486 (made in Vietnam)
            were included.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The hazard.</strong> The CPSC notice says the treadmills could unexpectedly
            accelerate, change speed or stop without user input, posing a fall hazard. Horizon had
            received at least 874 reports of this, including 71 reports of injury, among them bruises,
            abrasions and two broken bones.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The remedy.</strong> Owners were told to stop using the recalled treadmill
            immediately and contact Horizon for a free USB drive containing a software repair. The CPSC
            describes the remedy as a repair, not a refund or replacement. Horizon's site carries a
            Safety Notices section for recall information.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The 2026 penalty.</strong> On 4 August 2026 the CPSC announced that Johnson Health
            Tech Trading had agreed to pay a $16.875 million civil penalty, resolving the agency's charges
            that it knowingly failed to report immediately that the T101-05 contained a defect that could
            create a substantial product hazard. The CPSC described the settlement as provisionally
            accepted, subject to public comment. Its release states that the company received the 874
            reports between March 2018 and October 2022, which is to say before the recall was announced.
            The acting chairman's statement noted that the firm had previously paid two civil penalties
            for similar reporting failures; one, announced by the CPSC in 2005, was a $500,000 penalty
            against Johnson Health Tech and Horizon Fitness for failing to report treadmill incidents.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it means for a buyer now.</strong> The recall is defined by model and serial
            number. The current T101 Connect is a later model and is not part of it. The practical risk
            is a used or old-stock T101-05 whose owner never installed the update. If you are offered
            one, check the serial number on the frame, ask whether the update was applied, and contact
            Horizon if you are unsure. Our
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101 review</a>
            covers the model's history, and we found no CPSC recall for the 7.0 AT, 7.4 AT, 7.8 AT or
            T202 when the roundup was checked.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recalls happen across the industry, and a recall that was announced and fixed is, in one
            sense, the system working. The reporting penalty is a different matter, and it is fair to
            let it count against the company's record. Whether it should stop you buying a current
            Horizon is a judgement only you can make; what it should do is make you register the
            machine, so that you are contacted directly if anything is announced in future.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How Horizon Compares With the Brands It Is Shopped Against</h2>
<p>
            Horizon is usually cross-shopped against three or four names, and the comparison is less
            about which is "better" than about which approach suits you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against NordicTrack and ProForm.</strong> These brands build around iFIT, with large
            touchscreens and automatic speed and incline control during classes. That is a real feature
            if you use it, and an ongoing cost either way. Horizon is the opposite bet: no platform, an
            open radio, and the money spent on warranty instead. If you are unsure whether you would use
            classes, our assessment of
            <a href="/is-proform-a-good-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether ProForm is a good brand</a>
            sets out the platform model from the other side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against Sole.</strong> Sole is the other brand most often recommended to people who
            want a durable machine without a subscription, and unlike Horizon it publishes motor
            ratings for its treadmills. The two are close enough that the decision usually comes down to the
            specific models, prices and warranty terms on the day. Our
            <a href="/sole-vs-nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole vs NordicTrack comparison</a>
            explains Sole's approach against the platform brands.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against Bowflex.</strong> Since 2024 Bowflex has been a sister brand, and the Bowflex
            T6 is a close match for the Horizon 7.0 AT on the core figures. Compare them on price, console
            and the exact warranty terms rather than on the assumption that one is fundamentally better
            built.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against marketplace brands.</strong> Below about $700, Horizon's competition is
            mostly walking pads and folding machines from brands that exist mainly on Amazon. Those are
            often cheaper, but they rarely offer anything like a lifetime frame and motor warranty, and
            the company behind them may not be reachable in five years. That gap is where Horizon's
            reputation was built.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy a Horizon, and Who Should Not</h2>
<p>
            Pulling the strengths and weaknesses together gives a fairly clear picture of the buyer
            Horizon suits.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy a Horizon if</strong> you want a treadmill that works completely without a
            subscription; you are happy to use your own phone, tablet or watch for apps such as Zwift or
            the Peloton app; you value a long frame and motor warranty from a large, established
            manufacturer; and your needs sit within the range's ceilings — up to 12 mph, up to 15%
            incline, up to 375 lb. Walkers on a budget are well served by the Go Series; regular runners
            should start at the 7.0 AT.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong> you want classes to control the machine for you; you need
            decline or a steep incline for hiking practice; you weigh more than about 350 lb, since a
            rating is a ceiling rather than a comfortable operating point; you want a published motor
            rating to compare; or you want long labour cover, which Horizon does not offer on any
            current model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Think carefully if</strong> the parent company's reporting record matters to you. It
            is a fair thing to weigh. The product fix for the T101-05 was straightforward, the current
            range has no open recall that we found, and the machines are sold widely through the group's
            own stores and major retailers, so buying one is not a leap into the unknown. But it is a
            reason to register your machine with Horizon on the day it arrives and to keep the paperwork.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are buying used,</strong> do not assume the lifetime frame and motor cover
            transfers to you; treadmill warranties are often limited to the original purchaser, so
            check Horizon's terms before counting on it. Check the serial number
            against the recall, confirm the model is one Horizon still supports with parts, and inspect
            the deck and belt as you would on any second-hand machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. If You Are Shopping for a Horizon</h2>
<p>
            If the answer to "is Horizon a good brand" is yes for you, the next decision is which
            Horizon, and the brand's tidy range makes that quick. Four things decide it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walk or run.</strong> Mostly walking points to the Go Series; running at all
            regularly points to the Studio Series and its 60-inch deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Weight rating with margin.</strong> Leave a margin of at least 50 lb between your
            weight and the maximum user weight; the ratings run from 300 lb on the T101 Connect to 375 lb
            on the 7.8 AT.</li>
            <li><strong>Parts cover.</strong> The jump from one year to three years of parts cover between
            the T202 and the 7.0 AT is the single biggest warranty step in the range.</li>
            <li><strong>Stock and price.</strong> Buy at the sale price, never the reference price, and
            check the ship date before choosing a model that suits you less simply because it is in
            stock.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">current Horizon treadmills, ranked</a>
            takes those four factors model by model, with the confirmed deck sizes, ratings, warranty
            terms and the prices seen on Horizon's store, plus the older models to skip. If you are using
            the treadmill mainly for virtual running, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for Zwift</a>
            ranks two of the Studio models against the alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Horizon a good treadmill brand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home users who want a treadmill without a subscription, yes. Every current model has a lifetime frame and motor warranty and open Bluetooth app support. The catches are that Horizon does not publish motor ratings, parts and labour cover are much shorter than the headline, apps cannot control the machine, and its parent company paid a civil penalty in 2026 over late reporting of a 2022 recall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Horizon treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon Fitness is owned by Johnson Health Tech, a fitness equipment manufacturer headquartered in Taichung, Taiwan, with its North American headquarters in Cottage Grove, Wisconsin. The same group owns the commercial Matrix brand, Vision and, since 2024, Bowflex, and runs the Johnson Fitness and Wellness store chain that sells several of these brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Horizon treadmills need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Horizon has no content platform. Every current model works fully with its own console, programs and dial controls, and sends speed and incline over Bluetooth FTMS to apps such as Zwift and the Peloton app on your own device. Those apps may charge their own fees, but the treadmill needs none of them, and the apps cannot change the incline for you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Was there a Horizon treadmill recall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. In October 2022 about 192,000 Horizon T101-05 treadmills with serial numbers beginning TM734 or TM486 were recalled in the US because they could accelerate, change speed or stop unexpectedly. The remedy was a free software update on a USB drive from Horizon. The current T101 Connect is a later model and is not part of that recall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long is the Horizon treadmill warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every current Horizon has a lifetime frame and motor warranty for home use. Parts cover is one year on the T101 Connect and T202, three years on the 7.0 AT and five years on the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT. Labour is one year on most models and two on the top two. Parts and labour are the terms most likely to matter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What horsepower are Horizon treadmill motors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon does not publish a horsepower or continuous-duty rating for any current treadmill. It names its drive systems and lists incline motor thrust, but not motor power. Reviewers quote figures between 2.5 and 4.0 horsepower across the range, which we could not confirm with Horizon. Judge the machines on deck size, weight rating and warranty instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Judging Horizon as a Brand</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from taking one headline — the warranty, the price or the recall — and letting it stand for the whole brand.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading "lifetime warranty" as lifetime repairs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The lifetime term covers the frame and motor, which rarely fail. The parts that usually do fail in the first few years are covered for one to five years, and labour for one or two. Compare the parts and labour terms between models, because that is where the real difference in ownership cost lies.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an old T101 without checking the serial</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Recalled T101-05 units have serial numbers beginning TM734 or TM486, and a second-hand seller may never have installed the free software update. Check the serial number on the frame before you pay, ask whether the update was applied, and contact Horizon if you cannot confirm it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting apps to run the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon treadmills send data to Zwift, Peloton and similar apps but cannot be controlled by them. Buyers who expected the incline to follow a class are the most common source of disappointment. If automatic control matters, choose a platform machine and budget for the subscription.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing motors on figures Horizon does not publish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horsepower numbers attached to Horizon models on listings and review sites are not Horizon's figures. Comparing them against another brand's published continuous rating compares a guess with a specification. Judge a Horizon on its deck, weight rating and warranty, and treat any motor figure as unconfirmed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

